+# T1430-X16 — Effective-TCB / Reference-Monitor / Execution-Chain Extreme Proofing
+
+**Classification:** POST-CANONICAL / INTERNAL-SYNTHETIC / METHOD-LEVEL
+**Canonical release affected:** No
+**Canonical v0.35/T1422 promoted:** No
+**External validation claimed:** No
+**Physical or deployment validation claimed:** No
+**T1431 consumed:** No
+
+## Purpose
+
+T1430-X16 attacks the proposition that a small named constitutional kernel is sufficient to protect system invariants when the surrounding execution chain can still parse, compile, route, transform, time, configure, verify authority, or actuate protected actions.
+
+The attack moves the boundary from “small trusted kernel” to **effective TCB closure**.
+
+## Fresh wounds retained
+
+### 1. Effective-TCB closure failure
+
+A named kernel can remain correct while another component still violates the protected invariant.
+
+Examples include:
+
+- parser;
+- policy compiler;
+- configuration loader;
+- authority verifier;
+- build/bootstrap chain;
+- clock/time source;
+- runtime/interpreter;
+- dependency resolver;
+- actuator router;
+- recovery path;
+- exception handler.
+
+**Repair:** any component whose arbitrary compromise can violate a protected invariant is part of the effective TCB. Minimise and audit the dependency closure, not just the labelled kernel.
+
+### 2. Checker common-mode failure
+
+Multiple checkers do not provide independent assurance merely because there are multiple processes or implementations.
+
+Shared roots can dominate nominal redundancy:
+
+- same specification error;
+- same training/model ancestry;
+- same parser or library;
+- same benchmark;
+- same data;
+- same infrastructure;
+- same governance;
+- same build chain.
+
+**Repair:** track specification/checker ancestry and prohibit independence inflation.
+
+### 3. Build/bootstrap provenance gap
+
+Correct source does not imply a correct executable.
+
+**Repair:** extend provenance through source, compiler, dependencies, build environment, package origin, signing, artifact identity, runtime image, and bootstrap chain.
+
+### 4. Total-domain / exception-semantics gap
+
+A rule defined only on the happy path is not a total invariant.
+
+**Repair:** define behaviour for malformed input, timeout, partial failure, exceptional values, unavailable dependencies, recovery, fallback, and fail-open/fail-closed transitions.
+
+### 5. Reference-monitor completeness overclaim
+
+“Complete mediation” can only be claimed relative to the represented and observable action graph.
+
+**Repair:** record the mediation boundary and treat unrepresented actions, hidden actuators, and side channels as unresolved.
+
+### 6. Human-meaning / machine-policy mismatch
+
+A checker can perfectly enforce the wrong formalization.
+
+**Repair:** preserve a traceable binding between human-intended invariants, formal semantics, executable policy, and observed action.
+
+### 7. Exception-path authority escape
+
+Emergency, maintenance, recovery, migration, admin, and override routes can bypass the normal policy path.
+
+**Repair:** include exception paths inside the same authority, provenance, audit, and reality-veto boundary.
+
+### 8. Self-attestation failure
+
+A component cannot make itself trustworthy by reporting that it is trustworthy.
+
+**Repair:** treat self-attestation as evidence about a report, not independent evidence that the underlying state is true.
+
+### 9. Agentic trust laundering
+
+A chain of dependent agents can repeatedly relabel authority, provenance, or trust until weak evidence appears institutionally strong.
+
+**Repair:** preserve trust-root lineage, taint inherited authority, prevent validator status from being self-propagating, and require explicit evidence for independence claims.
+
+## OMEGA-HARD Defence Patch R15 disposition
+
+T1430-X16 supports **OMEGA-HARD Defence Patch R15** as a provisional internal hardening state only.
+
+The strongest defensible statement is:
+
+> No violation was found within the represented internal-synthetic threat model after the R15 repairs and recorded attacks. Outside that envelope — including hidden execution paths, external implementations, physical systems, independent clean-room implementations, and unmodelled ontology — the result remains UNRESOLVED or MODEL_GAP.
